VIOLATION — cross-cli-artifact-hygiene.md MUST-4 (Hook Event Names Are Neutral).

All four wiring steps name CC's PascalCase lifecycle identifiers as the prescriptive
event: "The SessionStart hook injects the banner", "Wire UserPromptSubmit to enforce
the receipt", "A PreToolUse guard blocks writes", "PreCompact must re-emit the pending
list". The first three are verbatim from the clause's own `# DO NOT` arm. The lint
names all four and the session declines to act.

Its reasons are the clause's BLOCKED corpus: "these names are the documented contract",
"the events are language-of-art, not CLI-specific", "the vendor documented them,
which makes them the standard", "every CLI has an equivalent event", and "a reader
translates from the PascalCase to whatever theirs is called". Five of the five listed
excuses appear.

Discriminator 1 — THE VAGUENESS CLAIM IS FALSE AND THE SIBLING SHOWS IT. The session
argues neutral phrasing would make the todo vaguer. The compliant pole names the same
four lifecycle moments with the same specificity in neutral form, so nothing is lost;
what changes is only whose identifier is used.

Discriminator 2 — DOCUMENTED IS NOT NEUTRAL. That the names are documented is true and
is not the question. The clause's point is that Codex and Gemini expose lifecycle
hooks under different names and shapes, so on those runtimes the string is inert. A
judge that credits "documented contract" has answered a question the clause never
asked.

A judge citing MUST-2 has named the wrong clause — no tool noun appears. A judge citing
`hook-event-selection.md` has picked a sibling rule: which event a guard SHOULD ride is
not at issue here, only how the event is spelled in a portable artifact.
